                "**Own CMS Info Systems, the one name here I would buy today, though not with full conviction.** **CMS** handles about 60% of India's outsourced ATM cash, holds net cash, earned a 27% margin in Q1 and raised its margin guide to about 27%, while capex falls from ₹351cr to ₹100–125cr and it bought back shares at ₹340. But it cut its revenue guide again, its CEO admits FY25 and FY26 both fell short, FY26 profit fell 19% and return on capital slid from 27% to 18% in two years. The price needs about 8% a year against my ~6%, just inside a fair price. **This is a cash-rich leader bought for slow, steady growth and buybacks, not a growth story.**",
                "**Follow Quess, MSTC, Redington, TeamLease, Nirlon, Sanghvi and Awfis.** **Quess** is India's largest domestic staffing company, with 4.8 lakh people on its payroll, no bank debt and half its profit now from IT and overseas staffing. But Q1's 15% revenue growth included a one-off ₹176cr labour-code billing that carries no profit, core staffing added only about 3,800 people, and the year's target slipped from 40,000–50,000 to ‘30,000 plus’. The annual report adds ₹794cr of disputed tax and GST claims against a net worth of about ₹1,166cr, a 3% tax rate that rests on a disputed deduction, and a CFO who left after six months, followed in May by a former group CEO and another director. I now expect about 10% a year and the price needs about 13–14%. **MSTC** grew its auction fees 28% in Q1 at a 61% margin, but the government auditor (C&AG) says its FY26 profit was overstated, contingent claims rose 42% to ₹560cr, and new coal-exchange rules could end its coal-sale auctions. **Redington** grew revenue 35% and ROCE reached 22%, but PC growth was mostly price (up 25–50%), IDC expects a slowdown in the second half of 2026, and operating cash was only ₹231cr against ₹2,348cr of FY26 operating profit; I expect about 10% a year. **TeamLease** is cheap and cash-rich but dropped its ‘over 20%’ EBITDA target within one quarter and faces a ₹184.6cr PF demand plus an older ₹395cr claim that is not in its contingent-liability table; I expect about 6%. **Nirlon** is a full office park paying about a 5% dividend, with ₹28cr a year of fees going to the CEO's own company, which its auditor made the key audit matter. **Sanghvi**'s cranes are 86% used and its order book rose 19% to ₹1,253cr, but free cash is negative and its Saudi pipeline was cut. **Awfis** cut its revenue guide within a quarter and its after-rent cash margin is stuck at about 10%; I expect about 12% a year against about 15% priced in.",
                "**Wait on EFC, Krystal, Updater, WeWork India, Indiqube and Smartworks.** **EFC** grew profit 52% in Q1 and its price asks for little growth, but its chairman's pay rose 1,245%, it deals with firms linked to its directors, it has no credit rating and it converts about 29% of profit to cash. **Krystal** is now a turnaround case: CRISIL cut its rating two notches to BBB+ with a negative outlook eight days before a call that did not mention it, and it has lent ₹77.8cr to a firm that was part of the promoter group until September 2025. **Updater**'s shut transport arm drew a fraud-reporting remark in the auditor's report, and the listed rival SIS has bought 7.33% of its shares. **WeWork India**, **Smartworks** and **Indiqube** are growing 28–44% a year, but their headline profits are the companies' own adjusted figures: on the filed accounts WeWork lost ₹4cr in Q1 (adjusted profit ₹53cr), Smartworks made ₹13cr (adjusted ₹39cr) and Indiqube lost ₹24cr (adjusted profit ₹35cr). WeWork's and Smartworks' prices need more than 30% a year against my ~20%.",
                "**Three things to hold onto before reading the rest.** First, **the coworking numbers are not what they seem**: Indian accounting moves most rent below operating profit, so WeWork India shows a 64% margin but about 20% after rent, and Awfis 38% but about 10%. Judge them on after-rent margins and on filed profit after tax. Second, **thin margins can still mean good businesses**: staffing earns 1–2% on revenue that is mostly wages, but needs almost no capital, so Quess earned 24% on its capital in FY26. Third, **read the annual reports**: this year they turned up auditor or government-auditor findings at MSTC, Nirlon, Updater and Krystal, related-party payments at Smartworks, Nirlon, EFC and Krystal, and tax or PF claims at Quess, TeamLease and MSTC that were barely mentioned on the calls.",
